# Metrics declared in construct-metrics that nothing in the workspace writes.
#
# Checked by scripts/check-observability.py. This is a RATCHET, not a budget:
# a new unwritten metric fails the check, and so does leaving a name here after
# it gains a producer. The list may only shrink.
#
# Why they exist: the Grafana overview was built on them. Nineteen of its 27
# panels read "No data" — indistinguishable on screen from an outage — because
# the metric behind them is a declaration and nothing more. Established
# 2026-08-13 with 16 entries.
#
# Each line: metric name, then why it is still here.

# ── Sessions. E2EE session setup happens between clients; the server relays
#    sealed payloads and cannot see init, healing or END_SESSION as events. To
#    instrument these at all, the client would have to report them — which is
#    telemetry, and the product promises none. Most likely these should be
#    DELETED rather than wired.
construct_session_init_success_total
construct_session_init_failure_total
construct_session_heal_attempts_total
construct_session_heal_success_total
construct_end_session_sent_total

# ── OTPK inventory. Genuinely worth having: running out of one-time prekeys
#    breaks new sessions for everyone contacting that device, and it fails
#    quietly. key-service knows both numbers at upload and consume time — this
#    is the strongest candidate on the list.
construct_otpk_remaining
construct_otpk_uploaded_total
construct_otpk_consumed_total

# ── Connected clients. GRPC_STREAMS_ACTIVE would answer "how many devices are
#    online right now", which nothing currently answers, and reconnect rate is
#    the first symptom of transport trouble. Second candidate.
construct_grpc_streams_active
construct_grpc_stream_reconnects_total

# ── Gateway request metrics. The only per-request latency and error view we
#    would have; the axum layer exists, nothing records into it. Third candidate.
gateway_requests_total
gateway_request_duration_seconds
gateway_circuit_breaker_state

# ── Key Transparency proof failures. KT verification is client-side; same
#    problem as the session metrics.
construct_kt_proof_failures_total

# ── TURN. Allocation counts live in coturn, not in our services; the right fix
#    is a coturn exporter, not writing these from Rust.
construct_turn_active_allocations
construct_calls_turn_relayed_total
